INTENT
Callers of the URL shortener who present a recognised API key in X-API-Key get their link-creation requests throttled against that key's own per-minute quota instead of the shared per-IP budget, while every other caller and every redirect keeps behaving exactly as it does today.

DESIGN
{
  "configuration": [
    {
      "default": "./data/links.db",
      "name": "LINKS_DB_PATH",
      "purpose": "Filesystem path to the SQLite database file (WAL mode). Tests and implementation must both use this exact name; DATABASE_PATH is not read."
    },
    {
      "default": "http://localhost:8000",
      "name": "LINKS_BASE_URL",
      "purpose": "Origin used to build the short_url field in the POST /api/links response. Trailing slash is stripped at parse time."
    },
    {
      "default": "7",
      "name": "LINKS_CODE_LENGTH",
      "purpose": "Number of base62 characters in a generated short code. Parsed as int; values outside 4..16 degrade to 7 with a warning."
    },
    {
      "default": "5",
      "name": "LINKS_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS",
      "purpose": "Bounded number of insert attempts when a generated code collides with the UNIQUE constraint before the request fails with 503 code_generation_failed."
    },
    {
      "default": "2048",
      "name": "LINKS_MAX_URL_LENGTH",
      "purpose": "Maximum accepted length of the target url in POST /api/links; longer bodies are 422 validation_error."
    },
    {
      "default": "0",
      "name": "LINKS_DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS",
      "purpose": "TTL applied when the request omits ttl_seconds. 0 means the link never expires (expires_at stored NULL)."
    },
    {
      "default": "31536000",
      "name": "LINKS_MAX_TTL_SECONDS",
      "purpose": "Upper bound accepted for ttl_seconds in POST /api/links; larger values are 422 validation_error."
    },
    {
      "default": "true",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED",
      "purpose": "Master switch for the rate-limit middleware. Parsed case-insensitively from {true,1,yes,on}. When false the middleware performs no accounting at all, for per-IP and for API-key buckets alike."
    },
    {
      "default": "10",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX",
      "purpose": "Requests per window allowed in the per-IP creation bucket, and the base for the redirect bucket (LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX * 100). Not applied to callers whose X-API-Key is a recognised key."
    },
    {
      "default": "60",
      "name": "LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS",
      "purpose": "Length of the fixed rate-limit window in seconds, shared by per-IP and per-key buckets. Also the upper clamp for Retry-After."
    },
    {
      "default": "10000",
      "name": "LINKS_MAX_TRACKED_KEYS",
      "purpose": "Maximum number of distinct per-IP limiter buckets retained in memory before LRU pruning. Applied independently and identically to the separate per-API-key bucket map, so key traffic cannot evict IP buckets."
    },
    {
      "default": "INFO",
      "name": "LINKS_LOG_LEVEL",
      "purpose": "Root log level for the application logger. At DEBUG the limiter logs bucket class and hashed bucket id only, never a key value or raw client address."
    },
    {
      "default": "",
      "name": "SHORTENER_API_KEYS",
      "purpose": "Comma-separated name:quota pairs, e.g. 'alpha:100,beta:20', giving each recognised API key its own per-window creation quota. Whitespace around names and quotas is trimmed. Read exactly once into the frozen Settings snapshot at application construction. Empty or unset means no keys configured and fully legacy behaviour. Note: no LINKS_ prefix."
    }
  ],
  "data_model": [
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, not null. Internal only; never exposed in any response, so it cannot be used to enumerate links.",
          "name": "id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, UNIQUE. The uniqueness of generated codes is enforced here and nowhere else; the writer inserts and catches IntegrityError rather than pre-checking.",
          "name": "code",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, length <= LINKS_MAX_URL_LENGTH, http/https absolute URL.",
          "name": "target_url",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, RFC3339 UTC string, set by the application at insert.",
          "name": "created_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULLABLE. NULL means never expires; otherwise RFC3339 UTC string compared lexicographically against now.",
          "name": "expires_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, monotonically incremented after each successful redirect.",
          "name": "click_count",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULLABLE, RFC3339 UTC string of the most recent redirect.",
          "name": "last_clicked_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "UNIQUE index on links(code) (implicit from the UNIQUE constraint) \u2014 the redirect hot path is a single equality lookup on code; without it every GET /{code} scans the table.",
        "No index on target_url: nothing queries by target, and indexing it would make 'has this URL been shortened' cheaply answerable.",
        "No new index is added by this change."
      ],
      "name": "links",
      "purpose": "One row per short link. Schema is frozen: app/db.py SCHEMA_STATEMENTS is unchanged by this work and PRAGMA table_info(links) must return exactly these columns."
    },
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, not null.",
          "name": "id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES links(id) ON DELETE CASCADE.",
          "name": "link_id",
          "type": "INTEGER"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NOT NULL, RFC3339 UTC string.",
          "name": "clicked_at",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULLABLE, truncated to 512 chars, taken from the Referer header.",
          "name": "referrer",
          "type": "TEXT"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "NULLABLE, truncated to 512 chars. No X-API-Key, X-Forwarded-For or peer address is ever copied into this row.",
          "name": "user_agent",
          "type": "TEXT"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Index on clicks(link_id) \u2014 the stats read path aggregates by link; without it stats scans the whole click log as it grows."
      ],
      "name": "clicks",
      "purpose": "Append-only click log backing click analytics. Frozen schema; PRAGMA table_info(clicks) is unchanged. Deliberately stores no client IP and no API key, so the acceptance check that database bytes contain no address and no key value holds by construction."
    },
    {
      "fields": [
        {
          "constraints": "Generated once per process at application construction, never logged, never persisted.",
          "name": "boot_salt",
          "type": "bytes (16 random bytes from secrets.token_bytes)"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Key is bucket_class + ':' + blake2s(boot_salt + client_host).hexdigest()[:16] where bucket_class is 'create' or 'redirect'. Capped at LINKS_MAX_TRACKED_KEYS entries with LRU eviction, exactly as today.",
          "name": "ip_buckets",
          "type": "OrderedDict[str, (window_start_epoch: float, count: int)]"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Key is 'create_key:' + blake2s(boot_salt + api_key_value).hexdigest()[:16]. Separately capped at LINKS_MAX_TRACKED_KEYS so eviction pressure here cannot evict ip_buckets entries. Bounded above in practice by the number of configured keys.",
          "name": "api_key_buckets",
          "type": "OrderedDict[str, (window_start_epoch: float, count: int)]"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Parsed once at construction from SHORTENER_API_KEYS. Keys are exact, case-sensitive, whitespace-trimmed names; values are ints > 0. Empty mapping when the variable is unset, empty or wholly unparsable.",
          "name": "api_key_quotas",
          "type": "Mapping[str, int] on the frozen Settings snapshot"
        },
        {
          "constraints": "Injectable monotonic time source (defaults to time.monotonic) so tests advance the window without sleeping.",
          "name": "clock",
          "type": "Callable[[], float]"
        }
      ],
      "indexes": [
        "Dict hash lookup by bucket id \u2014 O(1) on the hot path; no scan of the bucket maps occurs per request, only amortised LRU eviction at the tail."
      ],
      "name": "RateLimiterState (in-process only, not persisted)",
      "purpose": "Fixed-window counters held in the middleware instance behind the existing lock. Never serialised to SQLite, the WAL, or any file. Two structurally identical but separate maps so that key traffic and IP traffic prune independently."
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "choice": "If the trimmed header value is an exact, case-sensitive member of the configured quota map, the request is charged solely to that key's bucket at that key's quota and the per-IP creation bucket is not touched or incremented. Otherwise (header absent, empty, or an unrecognised value) the request is charged to the per-IP creation bucket at LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX exactly as today.",
      "decision": "Which bucket a creation request is charged to when X-API-Key is present",
      "rationale": "A partner holding 'alpha:100' behind one NAT address gets its full 100 creations even when LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX is 2, and an anonymous or misconfigured caller cannot buy extra budget just by inventing a header value.",
      "rejected": [
        "Charging both buckets (a key holder behind one IP would still be cut off at LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX, failing the 'five 201s with MAX=2' criterion).",
        "Rejecting unknown keys with 401/403 (turns a throttling feature into authentication, breaks callers who send a stale key, and is explicitly excluded by the criteria).",
        "Falling back to the highest configured quota for unknown keys (unbounded free budget for anyone who guesses the header name)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "In the existing ASGI middleware, ahead of routing and body parsing, keyed on a per-boot salted blake2s digest of the header value; the middleware inspects only the raw ASGI headers list and never reads the request body.",
      "decision": "Where API-key throttling is enforced",
      "rationale": "A throttled request never opens a database connection or parses a payload, so a flood costs almost nothing and writes no links row, which is exactly what the 429 criterion asserts.",
      "rejected": [
        "A FastAPI dependency on the route (runs after routing and body parsing, so a throttled 100KB body is still read and validated, and the error shape becomes FastAPI's rather than the stable envelope).",
        "A reverse-proxy/nginx limit (cannot see per-key quotas from SHORTENER_API_KEYS and does not exist in the single-process test harness).",
        "Storing counters in SQLite (writes key-derived state to disk, violating the credential constraint, and turns every creation into an extra write)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "choice": "bucket_id = class_prefix + ':' + blake2s(boot_salt || value).hexdigest()[:16], with a 16-byte per-process random salt, applied identically to client addresses and API keys. Log records emit only the class prefix and the truncated digest; response bodies and headers never include the key.",
      "decision": "How limiter identity is derived so credentials never leak",
      "rationale": "A DEBUG log dump or a heap-adjacent artefact from one boot cannot be replayed to recover a key or an address, and the 429 body is byte-identical whether it came from a key bucket or an IP bucket, so an attacker cannot probe which keys exist.",
      "rejected": [
        "Using the raw key as the dict key (a repr in a traceback or DEBUG log would expose the credential).",
        "Unsalted SHA-256 (a rainbow table over short key names recover

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:100,beta:20", LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true and LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10, 100 consecutive POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: alpha from one client address all return 201, and the 101st within the same 60-second window returns 429 with body {"error":{"code":"rate_limited",...}} and writes no row to links.
- With the same configuration, 20 POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: beta return 201 and the 21st returns 429; a subsequent POST carrying X-API-Key: alpha in the same window still returns 201, proving key buckets are independent.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:100" and LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=2, five POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: alpha from a single client address all return 201, proving a known key is not additionally charged against the per-IP creation bucket.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:100" and LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10, POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: gamma (unknown) returns 201 for the first 10 requests from one address and 429 on the 11th; no request returns 401 or 403.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS set, POST /api/links carrying no X-API-Key header behaves byte-for-byte as today: 201 up to LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX per client key, then 429 with Retry-After.
- A keyed 429 response carries a Retry-After header whose value is a decimal string of whole seconds, is >= 1 and <= 60, contains no fractional part, and is accompanied by Cache-Control: no-store.
- After the 60-second window for an exhausted key has elapsed (simulated by advancing the limiter clock, not by sleeping), the next POST /api/links with that key returns 201.
- With alpha's creation quota exhausted, GET /{code} carrying X-API-Key: alpha still returns 307 with the stored Location, and remains limited only by the existing per-IP redirect bucket (LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX * 100 per window).
- GET /api/links/{code}/stats carrying X-API-Key: alpha returns 200 and is never rate limited, before or after the key's creation quota is exhausted.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS unset or empty, every request, including one carrying X-API-Key: alpha, produces exactly the status codes, headers and bodies the service produces today; the entire existing test suite passes unmodified.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha,beta:abc,gamma:0,:5,delta:20", the application starts, POST /api/links with X-API-Key: delta is limited at 20 per minute, and X-API-Key values alpha, beta, gamma fall back to the per-IP limit; no exception is raised and no malformed value appears in a response body.
- With SHORTENER_API_KEYS=" alpha : 100 , beta:20 ", X-API-Key: alpha is limited at 100 per minute (surrounding whitespace on names and quotas is trimmed).
- Key matching is exact and case-sensitive: with SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:100" and LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=2, the third POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: ALPHA from one address returns 429.
- With LINKS_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false and SHORTENER_API_KEYS="alpha:1", 15 POST /api/links carrying X-API-Key: alpha all return 201.
- After exercising the key limiter, PRAGMA table_info(links) and PRAGMA table_info(clicks) return exactly the columns they return today, no new table exists in the database, and the raw bytes of every file in the database directory contain neither the string "alpha" as a key value nor any client IP address.
- A 429 caused by the key limiter never echoes the presented X-API-Key value in its body or headers, and no log record emitted at LINKS_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG during the request contains the key value.

CONSTRAINTS
- Python with FastAPI 0.115.6 / Starlette 0.41.3 / pydantic 2.10.4 exactly as pinned in requirements.txt; no new runtime dependency.
- Environment variable name is exactly SHORTENER_API_KEYS (no LINKS_ prefix) and the request header is exactly X-API-Key (matched case-insensitively per HTTP header rules).
- No SQLite schema change: no new table, column or index; app/db.py SCHEMA_STATEMENTS must remain as-is.
- No new HTTP endpoint, no admin surface, and no change to the existing routes' contracts other than the added 429 condition on POST /api/links.
- All existing environment variables keep their current names, defaults, ranges and semantics; the existing tests in tests/ must pass unmodified.
- The existing per-IP throttle on GET /{code} (bucket 'redirect', REDIRECT_LIMIT_MULTIPLIER = 100) must remain byte-for-byte equivalent in behaviour; redirects are never limited by API key.
- Rate limiting continues to run as ASGI middleware ahead of routing and body parsing, so a throttled creation never reaches the database.
- API key values are credentials: they must never be written to SQLite, the WAL, any file or any log record, and must never appear in a response body or header; limiter state must key on a per-boot salted hash exactly as client addresses do today.
- Limiter state stays in-process, thread-safe under the existing lock, and bounded in memory; adding key buckets must not let per-IP bucket pruning (MAX_TRACKED_KEYS) regress.
- Error responses keep the stable envelope {"error":{"code":...,"message":...}}; a key-limited rejection uses status 429 with code "rate_limited" and a Retry-After header of whole seconds.
- Configuration parsing must fail safe as app/config.py already does: an unusable SHORTENER_API_KEYS value is logged and degraded to 'no keys configured' rather than raising at import or startup.
- Keys are read once from the environment into the frozen Settings snapshot at application construction, not re-read per request.

Implement this design as a complete, runnable service. Include requirements.txt with pinned versions. The next stage generates tests against the files you return and reviews them line by line, so return the code you would defend in that review.
